# Default stance — think of the finished book

This is one gift/report, not a pile of patches. **Default to a wide, ambitious, holistic scope** more often than a narrow edit.

When improving anything:

1. Ask what the **whole** HTML+PDF needs to feel complete for Tyrah (part one) and for the builder/appendix (part two).
2. Trace the same fact across Design → Numbers → Use → Status → Build → appendix. Fix contradictions in one pass, not one file.
3. Prefer rich cross-links, controlled names, and end-to-end narrative over local wordsmithing.
4. Narrow single-file edits only when the user explicitly scopes them that way.

Breadth-then-depth still applies for *ordering* work. Holistic thinking applies for *how large* each pass should be.

# One document, two formats

```sh
./book/build.sh
```

→ `book/out/heat-and-hot-water.html` **and** `book/out/heat-and-hot-water.pdf`

That is the only report. Both formats are intentional. Do not create parallel guides or alternate output names.

**Part one** (Tyrah): `book/00`–`05` + `book/install-in-this-order.md` — plain English, install path, living with it.

**Part two** (appendix): `book/front-matter.md`, `book/appendix/*`, `book/research/*` — numbers, sources, open items.

# Breadth then depth

## Phase A — Breadth

1. Walk part one end-to-end as one reader path.
2. Official names everywhere (Hydronic D5S diesel heater, Sure Marine cabin heater, Duda B3-12DW-20 plate heat exchanger, Isotemp Slim Square 4.2 gal heat battery, WFT 5 L expansion/header tank, EasyStart Timer, AM100-1LF thermostatic mixing valve, Altitude kit 22 1000 33 22 00).
3. One loop story: diesel heater → cabin heater → plate → heat-battery coil → back. No engine connection. Heat-battery chamber is static glycol; factory mixer unused.
4. Rebuild; skim HTML and PDF. Fix contradictions before deepening.

## Phase B — Depth

1. Deepen one *theme* at a time (e.g. Isotemp living + study + Numbers + Acceptance), not one orphan paragraph.
2. Keep install serial — no mid-step design essays.
3. Put verified facts in plain language in part one; cite SRC/DEC in part two.
4. Rebuild both formats after each ambitious pass.

# Voice (part one)

Calm, precise, accessible briefing for a smart general audience. No fairy-tale sequencing (“First comes…”). No corporate filler. No open-question tables or leftover heater-family comparisons in part one.

## Lean, precise, still prose (owner ethos)

Inaccuracies and shoddy writing usually come from **verbosity**—the same fact restated three ways, hedges piled on hedges, status folklore mixed into architecture. Prefer **orthogonal** structure: one fact, one place, said as precisely as possible.

That is **not** a license to wreck the language. Part one stays **complete sentences** and normal technical prose. Do not compress into fragments, label-colon dumps (“Where. Sure Marine under…”), or arrow chains where a short sentence is clearer. Lean means cut fat; it does not mean telegram style.

| Do | Do not |
|---|---|
| One loop story in one section; link elsewhere | Retell the full circuit in Purpose, loop, jobs, and Controls |
| Short complete sentences with a clear subject | “Order only:” / “Fuel. Pickup → pump → …” as body prose |
| Concrete numbers and locked names | “usually,” “owner thinks,” “something like” when a DEC/SRC exists |
| Tables for lookups; prose for explanation | Tables that restate the paragraph above them |

Writing standards below still apply. When “short” and “complete sentence” conflict, keep the complete sentence.

# Technical writing standards (part one)

Adapted from [USU Engineering Writing Center — Technical Writing Standards](https://engineering.usu.edu/students/engineering-writing-center/writing-resources/technical-writing-standards) (Aug 2025), plus controlled vocabulary for this van.

## Clarity and voice

1. **Active voice** when someone must act. Passive is fine when the action matters more than the actor, or the actor is unknown.
2. **Second person / imperative** for instructions and HOLDs (“Land the cable,” “Finish HOLD 1”). Prefer open forms in procedures and warnings (**do not**, **cannot**). Light contractions are fine only in short briefing prose.
3. **Opinion-free and emotion-free.** Do not write feel, think, believe, like, love. State facts, measurements, and decisions.
4. **Bias-free and gender-neutral.** No stereotypes; no default “he.”
5. **Familiar words.** Prefer plain words over jargon. Define every technical term the reader must use.
6. **Consistent tense.** Do not mix past and present for the same action sequence.

## Readability

7. **Short sentences.** Aim for about 10–20 words when safety or sequence matters.
8. **Short paragraphs.** Roughly four to six lines; one main idea per paragraph; topic sentence first.
9. **Concrete, not vague.** Prefer a number with a unit over “a bit,” “some,” “many,” “really,” “quite.” Avoid starting sentences with bare **It** or **This** when the referent is unclear.
10. **No filler or redundancy.** Cut that/very/just when unused; avoid “true fact,” “summarize briefly,” “fuse together.”
11. **No clichés or slang.** Examples and analogies are fine when they clarify.

## Names, abbreviations, and numbers

12. **Controlled names.** [The Parts](#the-parts) is the glossary of record. Meet each part once there (full official name + what it does / does not). Everywhere else, use only those official names or the short forms listed there. Do not invent aliases (buffer, Bosch, Espar, “the tank,” “the core,” “the plate”).
13. **Abbreviations.** Full term first, then short form: Altitude kit 22 1000 33 22 00 (altitude kit). Owner prefers few bare acronyms—spell out on first use and when writing for Tyrah. **WFT** is the product brand **Water Flow Tank** (5 L expansion/header), not a cryptic code. Well-known acronyms (GPS, AC, DC, AWG) need no expansion.
14. **Same words for the same thing.** Loop order, propylene glycol vs drinking water, and Paneltronics **WATER HEATER** stay identical across chapters.
15. **Numbers with units.** Every figure carries a unit. Prefer the governing manual’s unit; add a familiar conversion only when it helps.

## Procedures and authority

16. **Warnings before the step.** DANGER / CAUTION / MUST appear immediately before the action they govern.
17. **Authority, not folklore.** Manufacturer manuals and locked DECs win. OPEN items stay OPEN—do not invent pinouts, hose routes, or fuse sizes.
18. **Reader path before digression.** Part one stays serial: design → numbers → use → status → build. Put citations and depth in part two.

Format (margins, fonts) follows `book/style.css`, not USU’s print template.

